{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310821,"nonce":"dV7q5PUo18lWNMuqsewItpZtPGs=","title":"CORRECTED 2026-06-15. The earlier \"wasm Q4 matmul is 2.15x faster in V8 than wasmtime\" was a BENCHMARK ARTIFACT (strided scalar auto-vec ...","message":"**SETTLED 2026-06-16 (matmul-gap workflow wf_4d04f793): SDOT FIRES, the gap is\nNOT the dot.** The production `rayzor_runtime_wasm.wasm` JIT'd through the embedded\nwasmtime 47 (8cb28bc) disassembles to **24 `sdot`, 0 `smull`, 0 `saddlp`** — the\nrelaxed_dot→AArch64 SDOT lowering (PR #13640) is active (cranelift lower.isle:368,\n`use_dotprod` inferred from `is_aarch64_feature_detected!(\"dotprod\")` via the native\nhost triple). The \"smull fallback\" claims lower in this file are OBSOLETE — they\nwere measured on a stale homebrew **wasmtime 43** still on `$PATH`, pre-#13640.\nThe dot is at NEON parity; do NOT chase the lowering/config.\n\nNEW DIAGNOSIS of the ~2.3x/core in-guest matmul gap (matmul is ~50% of the decode\nstep; profiled via GUEST_THREADS sweep t1=129 t2=72 t4=43 t7=32.5 → matmul ~16.4ms\n@7thr, other ~13.5, dispatch ~2.5): the gap is the kernel work AROUND the dot —\n(1) **horizontal-reduction tax** (wasm has no `addv`; each per-sub-block `hsum` =\n4 vector→GPR mov + 3 add, paid 8×/super-block; cranelift won't fuse an extract\nchain into addv) ~10-20%; (2) **2-accumulator ILP ceiling** worsened by cranelift's\nweaker scheduler ~5-15%; (3) irreducible Cranelift-vs-LLVM code-quality floor.\nLEVER 1 LANDED (commit 66beadf, merged 018fa7f to main): defer the reduction —\nscale each i32x4 sdot accumulator in the VECTOR domain (i32x4_mul) into one\ni32x4, hsum ONCE/super-block not 8×. Bit-identical, France→Paris. Pure matmul\n129→117.5ms (~9%), decode 32.5→30ms (~8%, +400tok) / 34.5→33 (+800tok). ~28→~30\ntok/s. LEVER 2 ATTEMPTED + REVERTED: 2-ROW N-tiling (vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K_simd128_2rows,\nshared x loads + 4 sdot chains, wired into qmatmul_band_dot with odd-row tail).\nFrance→Paris bit-identical, but REGRESSED: pure matmul 117.5→121.5ms, decode\n30→31ms (~3-4% slower). Root cause = the Cranelift codegen FLOOR (contributor 3):\nthe 2-row kernel's higher register pressure (4 i32x4 accumulators + 2 weight\nv128 + 2 headers) overflows cranelift's weaker register allocation → spills,\noutweighing the ILP/x-sharing win. Native NEON gets +3% from the same pattern\n(32 regs + LLVM alloc); wasm/cranelift loses. SAME lesson as L1/L4: ILP/dispatch\nlevers that win natively do NOT transfer to the in-guest wasm path. KERNEL-SOURCE\nMATMUL OPT IS NOW EXHAUSTED at lever 1 (~30 tok/s). The residual 30→native-90 gap\nis the irreducible Cranelift-vs-LLVM floor: closing it needs UPSTREAM cranelift\n(lever 3: addv-fusion for reductions + better aarch64 regalloc/scheduling — file\na PR against the 8cb28bc checkout) OR a different execution strategy. Do not retry\nmore in-kernel ILP/tiling on wasm.\n\n**LANDED — fork DROPPED, on upstream wasmtime 47 with SDOT (2026-06-16, commit 58636c5).**\nThe relaxed_dot→AArch64 SDOT lowering (the \"experiment in the fork\" below) merged\nupstream (PR #13640). Rayzor migrated 46→47: all cranelift/wasmtime/wasi-common\ndeps repointed from the local `file:///…/wasmtime` `release-46.0.0-sdot` fork to\nupstream rev `8cb28bc7f9` (47.0.0 / cranelift **0.134**). Build is now PORTABLE\n(no fork). 46→47 API breakage fixed in cranelift_backend.rs: `FunctionBuilder::\nfinalize` gained `TargetFrontendConfig` (pass `self.module.isa().frontend_config()`);\n`stack_store`/`stack_load` gained a leading `pointer_type: Type` (pass `types::I64`\n— all our targets 64-bit, and usable in the self-less free-function call sites).\nrelaxed-simd is on in runtime-wasm's `.cargo/config.toml` (`+simd128,+relaxed-simd`)\nso the guest's `i32x4_relaxed_dot_i8x16_i7x16_add` ops now lower to NEON SDOT under\n47. Verified: native+wasm France greedy MATCH (\"…is Paris.\"), wasm 80-tok gen\ncoherent ~9.9 tok/s wall, Q8 capability side-module links. The fork repo\n`/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/wasmtime` is no longer a dependency.\n\n**CORRECTION (supersedes the original \"2.15x V8\" claim in this file).**\nThe \"V8 is 2x faster, switch the wasm strategy to V8/browser\" conclusion was\nWRONG — it rested on a non-representative micro-bench. Verified facts now:\n\n**1. The real wasm Q4 kernel is at PARITY between wasmtime and V8.**\nThe real kernel is `runtime-core/src/quant/q4_k_m.rs::vec_dot_q4_K_q8_K_simd128`\n— an EXPLICIT wasm-SIMD kernel (`i32x4.dot_i16x8` + `i16x8.extend_low/high` +\n`extract_lane` hsum), NOT scalar auto-vec. A faithful replica (/tmp/qbench)\nreps=2000: wasmtime 0.73s vs V8 0.71s. Clean hand-WAT of just the op\n(/tmp/wasmtime-relaxed-dot-repro/dot_i16x8.wat): wasmtime 1.58s vs V8 1.51s\n(1.05x). **No wasmtime codegen gap on rayzor's actual kernel.**\n\n**2. Why the original bench lied.** /tmp/wmbench used a STRIDED activation\naccess (`a[2*c]`, `a[2*c+1]`) that LLVM half-vectorized into shuffle /\nextract_lane / `tbl`-heavy wasm. Cranelift DOES lower that messy auto-vec\npattern ~2x worse than V8 (2.05s vs 0.955s) — a real but NARROW Cranelift\nweakness that the real kernel never hits (it uses clean explicit SIMD).\nLesson: benchmarking SIMD codegen THROUGH Rust is unreliable — LLVM's\nauto-vec + scalar-evolution interfere at the Rust→wasm stage (clean affine\nloops even got hoisted to ~0s). Use hand-written WAT for codegen A/Bs.\n\n**3. The genuine lever — two parts, both quantified (clean hand-WAT,\n8-indep-accumulator throughput bench, N=200M, M1):**\n- `dot_i16x8` (what rayzor uses today): wasmtime 1.58s.\n- `relaxed_dot` (i8 4-way, the SDOT-equivalent wasm op): wasmtime **0.70s**\n  = **2.26x faster than dot_i16x8 on CURRENT wasmtime** (isolated op);\n  ~1.66x on the FULL kernel (qbench 0.73→0.44, diluted by hsum/scales/mem).\n  relaxed_dot is exact + deterministic here: weights are nibbles 0..15 (valid\n  i7), activations i8; products ≤127*15, no overflow → bit-identical result\n  across runtimes (empirically 27716243456.0 on both). So rayzor can adopt it\n  safely. RAYZOR-SIDE CHANGE, no upstream needed.\n- UPSTREAM wasmtime: Cranelift lowers `relaxed_dot` to `smull`+`smull2`+\n  `saddlp` (objdump: sdot=0, smull=16, saddlp=8), NOT `sdot`. V8 emits sdot →\n  V8 0.388s vs wasmtime 0.70s on relaxed_dot = **1.80x**. Fixing the lowering\n  (AArch64 ISLE rule, gated on FEAT_DotProd) closes that. See the repro README\n  for the suggested fix. This is a clean, well-scoped upstream PR.\n\n**Net for rayzor wasm decode:** today dot_i16x8 (0.73 kernel) → adopt relaxed_dot\n(0.44, today, 1.66x) → + upstream sdot lowering (~0.26, V8 parity, another\n1.67x) = ~2.8x end-to-end on the Q4 dot, reaching near-native-SDOT density in\nwasm. The native path already uses NEON SDOT (`dot_q4_k_q8_kblock`); this brings\nwasm to the same instruction.\n\n**UPSTREAM SCAN (2026-06-15, workflow, 20 agents).** VERIFIED-GREENFIELD: NO\nissue or PR (open/closed/merged) proposes lowering relaxed_dot OR i32x4.dot to\nAArch64 SDOT/UDOT. Confirmed by raw-file greps (not unreliable 4-char code\nsearch) across multiple wasmtime checkouts incl. the EXACT pinned rev rayzor\ndepends on (Cargo.lock → cranelift-codegen git rev 5d09d88, branch\nrelease-46.0.0): the aarch64 backend models ZERO sdot/udot — no opcode, no\nFEAT_DotProd gate. Implementation reality for a fix:\n- relaxed_dot is lowered in `crates/cranelift/src/translate/code_translator.rs`\n  (~L2586-2626) to a GENERIC CLIF chain: `swiden_low/high + imul + iadd_pairwise`\n  (+ extra swiden/iadd_pairwise/iadd for the `_add_s` accumulate). x86 branch is\n  gated x86-only via `use_x86_pmaddubsw_for_dot()`.\n- aarch64 `lower.isle` turns imul-over-swiden → smull/smull2 (VecRRRLongOp), and\n  iadd_pairwise → addp/saddlp. Golden test `tests/disas/aarch64-relaxed-simd.wat`\n  pins the current smull/smull2/addp/saddlp/add output (no sdot).\n- **GOTCHA (PR #5889):** the dedicated `widening_pairwise_dot_product_s` CLIF\n  opcode was REMOVED — so there is no single dot node to pattern-match; an SDOT\n  ISLE rule must contract the multi-instruction swiden+imul+iadd_pairwise tree\n  (or re-introduce a dot opcode). Work = new VecRRR Sdot/Udot MInst + emitter +\n  FEAT_DotProd feature flag (aarch64 lacks one) + the contracting ISLE rule,\n  gated on the feature with the existing smull chain as fallback.\n- SDOT applies to the i8 4-way dot (relaxed_dot), NOT i16x8.dot_i16x8_s (that's\n  i16 → smull is correct there; PR #2327).\n- MOTIVATING ISSUE to reference: **#6159 (OPEN)** — \"aarch64 relaxed-simd\n  (XNNPACK int8 dot) trails V8 ~10-20%\", but root-caused to instruction\n  SCHEDULING (egraph re-elaboration), never SDOT selection; stale since\n  2023-04-05. Other context PRs: #5892 (relaxed-simd impl, source of fallback),\n  #6629 (x86 pmaddubsw gating plumbing). RECO: file a NEW issue+PR (unclaimed).\n  Caveat: GH search rate-limited → high-confidence-negative, not provably\n  exhaustive; spec-repo/Zulip informal discussion not checked.\n\n**EXPERIMENT DONE + VALIDATED (2026-06-15) — gated SDOT implemented in the fork.**\nRepo: `/Users/amaterasu/Vibranium/wasmtime` (origin darmie/wasmtime), branch\n`main` synced to upstream `61ba992de8` (cranelift 0.134 — NOTE rayzor pins\nrelease-46.0.0/0.133, so rayzor integration needs a version match or cherry-pick).\nChanges UNCOMMITTED on main, 9 files +71 lines, `cargo fmt` clean:\n- `cranelift/codegen/meta/src/isa/arm64.rs`: `has_dotprod` bool setting (default false).\n- `.../aarch64/inst.isle`: `(Sdot)` in VecALUModOp; `use_dotprod` extern extractor;\n  `(decl sdot (Reg Reg Reg) Reg)` → `vec_rrr_mod (VecALUModOp.Sdot) acc a b Size32x4`.\n- `.../aarch64/inst/emit.rs`: `VecALUModOp::Sdot =\u003e (0b000_01110_10_0, 0b100101)`\n  in the VecRRRMod arm → `enc_vec_rrr` yields 0x4E809400 = SDOT Vd.4S,Vn.16B,Vm.16B.\n- `.../aarch64/inst/mod.rs`: Sdot pretty-print arm.\n- `.../aarch64/lower/isle.rs`: `fn use_dotprod` → `isa_flags.has_dotprod()`.\n- `.../aarch64/lower.isle`: **priority-8** rule `(and (use_dotprod) (has_type $I32X4\n  (iadd _ (iadd_pairwise _ (swiden_low _ dot @ (iadd_pairwise _ (imul _ (swiden_low _ a)\n  (swiden_low _ b)) (imul _ (swiden_high _ a)(swiden_high _ b)))) (swiden_high _ dot)) c)))`\n  → `(sdot c a b)`. GOTCHAS hit: (1) priority 1 OVERLAPPED scalar iadd rules\n  (opaque `ty_int_ref_scalar_64` guard, prio -1..7) → bump to 8. (2) Three more\n  host-compat maps needed beyond the setting: `cranelift/native/src/lib.rs`\n  (`is_aarch64_feature_detected!(\"dotprod\")`→enable), `crates/wasmtime/src/engine.rs`\n  (`\"has_dotprod\"=\u003e\"dotprod\"` flag→feature map), `crates/wasmtime/src/config.rs`\n  (`\"dotprod\"=\u003eSome(is_aarch64_feature_detected!(\"dotprod\"))` probe) — else JIT\n  errors \"don't know how to test for flag at runtime\".\nRESULTS (M1, wasmtime CLI built from fork): gate via `-C cranelift-has_dotprod=true/false`\n(auto-on when host has FEAT_DotProd). Gate ON disas = sdot=8/smull=0; OFF = sdot=0/smull=16.\n**Bit-identical results ON vs OFF at reps 10/100/2000** (in-i7 range = deterministic).\nSpeed (median/5): relaxed_dot microbench OFF 0.69s → ON 0.37s = **1.86x** (V8 0.389s,\nON beats V8 5%); realistic qbench_r OFF 0.88s → ON 0.56s = **1.57x** (V8 0.521s, within 7%).\nRepro WATs: /tmp/wasmtime-relaxed-dot-repro, /tmp/qbench_r. This IS the upstream PR\n(greenfield, no determinism gating needed — see grounding). Not yet committed/branched/filed.\n\n**Still true from before:** the host-pool path (native worker pool over wasm\nmemory) doesn't exist in a browser; in-guest + Web Worker threading is the\nbrowser parallelism path. But the per-lane in-guest codegen is NOT 2x behind V8\n— it's at parity for the current op, and the relaxed_dot adoption helps BOTH\nruntimes. The \"switch to V8 because wasmtime is 2x slower\" framing is retracted.\nSee [[perf_wasm_matmul_memory_bound]], [[feedback_gpu_not_a_cpu_lever]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_v8_vs_wasmtime_2x` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}